 "findings": [
  "Run-to-run noise is small: the two shipped-config runs and the two step-4096 runs agree within about 2 % on every loaded metric (the first scenario of a run, short, has a warm-up effect: 180 ms against 142 ms).",
  "--performance-mode interactivity changed nothing measurable (decode 50.1 against 50.3 tokens/s, same loaded latencies) and took 83 s to become healthy against 58 s: not adopted.",
  "--max-num-batched-tokens 2048 equals the default behaviour (C = A).",
  "Priority scheduling with a 4096-token step, against the shipped configuration: evidence-sized first token 706 -> 550 ms (-22 %), 24k-token prefill 7.54 -> 6.80 s (-10 %), 8-way aggregate throughput 63.8 -> 69.4 tokens/s (+9 %), foreground first token beside four background requests 1.72 -> 1.13 s (-34 %) and its total 2.88 -> 2.25 s (-22 %); single-stream decode and long-context recall (3/3 at 8k and 24k tokens) unchanged; no preemption.",
  "A step of 8192 is no better than 4096 where it matters (evidence first token 592 ms, loaded foreground the same) and doubles the longest step a foreground request can wait behind: 4096 is adopted.",
  "A typical NeuroSciFi answer prompt is 1.1k tokens (p95 1.7k), under either step size: the step matters for long evidence, tool loops and concurrent tasks, not for the median task."
 ]
